WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:09.380
12 years. That is what insulin cost Frederick Sanger 51 amino acids, read one

2
00:00:09.380 --> 00:00:13.520
stubborn position at a time, when nobody knew whether reading them would even mean

3
00:00:13.520 --> 00:00:14.520
anything.

4
00:00:14.733 --> 00:00:19.953
In 1943 the field knew a protein was a chain of amino acids and knew the 20-odd

5
00:00:19.953 --> 00:00:21.093
letters of the alphabet.

6
00:00:21.653 --> 00:00:26.193
What nobody knew was whether the letters spelled a word, or whether each protein was

7
00:00:26.193 --> 00:00:27.453
a different random draw.

8
00:00:28.213 --> 00:00:29.213
Sanger settled it.

9
00:00:29.683 --> 00:00:34.003
When the sequence finally surfaced he wrote that proteins are definite chemical

10
00:00:34.003 --> 00:00:38.843
substances possessing a unique structure in which each position in the chain is

11
00:00:38.843 --> 00:00:44.763
occupied by one and only one amino acid residue—a single sentence that killed both

12
00:00:44.763 --> 00:00:48.543
the periodic pattern theory and the random mixture theory at once.

13
00:00:49.367 --> 00:00:54.327
He did not trust a result he could check only one way: because, as he put it, the

14
00:00:54.327 --> 00:00:59.467
methods used were new and were qualitative rather than quantitative, he gathered far

15
00:00:59.467 --> 00:01:04.307
more overlapping fragments than the bare logic required, cross-checking every

16
00:01:04.307 --> 00:01:07.227
conclusion from several directions before he would believe it.

17
00:01:07.900 --> 00:01:11.660
He was, by his own cheerful admission, academically not brilliant.

18
00:01:12.240 --> 00:01:16.540
He scrubbed hospital floors as a conscientious objector during the war and declined

19
00:01:16.540 --> 00:01:18.940
a knighthood because he did not want to be different.

20
00:01:19.640 --> 00:01:23.750
When the great genome center at Hinxton asked to take his name, he made one

21
00:01:23.750 --> 00:01:25.670
condition: it had better be good.
